# Line endings are load-bearing in this repository, so git converts nothing by default.
#
# scripts/check_pinned_paths.py verifies freeze receipts by RAW SHA-256 of exact bytes at exact paths,
# and several of those receipts are externally timestamped or OSF-registered. Raw bytes are the point of
# an attestation: normalising them would let two different byte sequences satisfy one record. But git's
# default end-of-line conversion (core.autocrlf) makes the working tree differ between platforms, which
# silently invalidates those digests -- a Linux checkout reported HASH CHANGED on four pinned files whose
# content was character-for-character identical, which reads like data corruption.
#
# The rule below removes the ambiguity: every file is stored and checked out exactly as committed, so a
# receipt written on one machine verifies on any other. Twelve prereg receipts genuinely contain CRLF in
# their committed bytes; a blanket `text=auto eol=lf` would rewrite those and break them.
* -text

# These three registries were attested while the working tree held CRLF, so their receipts record the
# CRLF digest, while their blobs are LF. They must therefore be materialised as CRLF for the recorded
# hash to verify. This is not a formatting preference -- it reproduces the exact bytes the receipt
# attests. Do not "tidy" them to LF without a dated amendment in prereg/amendments/ that re-verifies
# each digest.
config/protocol/national_state_zone_provenance.json  text eol=crlf
config/registries/national_state_zone_coverage.csv   text eol=crlf
config/registries/port_complex_crosswalk.csv         text eol=crlf
